{%- comment -%}
  The discount percentage, in one place.

  It was computed independently in three files — price.liquid, product-card.liquid
  and buy-box.liquid — and the three had already drifted: only buy-box carried a
  trailing `floor`. On Shopify they agree today because divided_by truncates two
  integers, so the drift was invisible rather than absent. The moment any input
  arrives as a float the card would print one number and the page beside it
  another, which is a defect a customer notices before a developer does.

  Outputs the integer and nothing else. It cannot assign a variable for its
  caller: `render` runs in an isolated scope, so anything assigned in here is
  invisible outside. Callers capture the output and coerce it back to a number:

    {%- capture saved_pct -%}{%- render 'saved-percent', variant: v -%}{%- endcapture -%}
    {%- assign saved_pct = saved_pct | plus: 0 -%}

  Emits 0 when there is no genuine discount, so a caller tests one number rather
  than repeating the comparison.

  floor is written out rather than left to divided_by. Shopify truncates when
  both sides are integers, which is the only reason this returns a whole number
  at all; stated explicitly it yields the identical figure and stops depending on
  the types of two values this snippet does not own.
{%- endcomment -%}
{%- liquid
  assign pct = 0
  if variant.compare_at_price > variant.price
    assign pct = variant.compare_at_price | minus: variant.price | times: 100 | divided_by: variant.compare_at_price | floor
  endif
  echo pct
-%}
